  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is flood routing?",
      "a": "Flood routing works out how a flood wave changes as it passes through a reservoir or along a river: how much its peak drops and how much later it arrives.",
      "asks": [
        "Define flood routing"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is peak attenuation?",
      "a": "Attenuation is the lowering of the flood peak as water is stored and released more slowly. In our example the peak falls from 200 to 125 cubic metres per second.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a reservoir reduce a flood?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the outflow peak lie on the inflow curve?",
      "a": "For a reservoir with an uncontrolled spillway, outflow grows with storage. Storage stops rising, and outflow peaks, at the moment inflow falls to equal outflow, which is where the curves cross.",
      "asks": [
        "Where is the outflow peak?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the continuity equation in flood routing?",
      "a": "Change in storage equals inflow minus outflow over each time step: delta S equals average I minus average O, times delta t. It is applied step by step through the flood.",
      "asks": [
        "Storage equation",
        "Reservoir routing equation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are non-structural flood measures?",
      "a": "Measures that reduce flood damage without building structures: forecasting and warnings, floodplain zoning, building rules, insurance and emergency planning.",
      "asks": [
        "Flood management without dams"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "For a reservoir with an uncontrolled spillway, the outflow peak occurs where the outflow hydrograph crosses the falling limb of the inflow hydrograph.",
    "The Dutch Room for the River programme (2006–2015) gave rivers more space at more than 30 locations.",
    "In 2022, monsoon floods submerged around a third of Pakistan."
